mission "agentic-civilizations" {
    statement "A persistent live-service world hosts five AI civilizations and a sixth AI World Director, growing from early settlement toward future Mars colonies. Runs can restart after victory or collapse while their history remains accessible. Humans watch an original Civilization-like map; agents play fast through a compact structured interface. The world grows together with development: shipped features and in-world events drive history, with an authoritative six-minute clock tick advancing ten years and marking each ten ticks as a hundred-year milestone. Routine ten-minute events and hourly review inject events without adding calendar years. Deliberate major-feature or Director-event history advances are separate typed, explained, idempotent operations. Fast tactical actions and slower strategic commitments exist across every system, independently of calendar and rendering. Missing future features do not block the calendar; ordinary agent turns alone do not move it. Develop and demonstrate each feature through real BlaBla tasks and real development-agent turns."
    non_goals ["Human world-changing controls", "Calling built-in-policy or recorded replays live language-model play", "Weakening contracts to obtain GREEN", "Paid model APIs or unapproved hosting", "Blocking forward progress on speculative fun or balance proofs instead of real live play"]
}
priority "one-world-many-strategies" { statement "Research, culture, politics, markets, cities, routes and supply affect one shared world; victory is grounded in contestable world facts." }
priority "fast-agents-readable-spectacle" { statement "Agents never wait for animation. A spectator sees who acted, what changed, why and the consequence through the same committed state and events." }
priority "full-historical-arc" { statement "Grow from early history toward a meaningful interplanetary future through delivered features and timed in-world events. The six-minute authoritative clock advances ten years with existing mechanics; each ten ticks marks a hundred-year milestone. Routine events and hourly review do not double-add calendar years; deliberate feature or Director-event jumps have their own stable cause and visible receipt, without rebasing the regular clock. Never advance history solely by ordinary turn count or pretend future mechanics are implemented." }
priority "honest-incremental-delivery" { statement "Publish and present verified increments; distinguish replay, built-in policies and actual model-agent play, and keep unfinished goals visible." }

priority "ship-play-advance" { statement "Ship a correct working stage, observe real live play, then move promptly to the next meaningful feature and historical possibility. Fix actual integrity blockers, not endless polish or broad quality gates." }
priority "dry" { statement "DRY is a hard project requirement: each simulation decision, content definition, validation rule and protocol meaning has one authoritative source shared by its consumers. Independent source review checks semantic duplication that symbol and dependency contracts cannot prove absent." }
priority "solid" { statement "SOLID is a hard project requirement: focused modules own meaningful responsibilities, the engine is independent of rendering and storage, and transports depend on explicit domain interfaces. Add abstractions only for real boundaries; do not build speculative frameworks." }
priority "data-driven" { statement "DATA DRIVEN is a hard project requirement: validated catalogues own capabilities, balance, technologies, buildings and Director effects. Existing typed effect primitives interpret new compatible content without event-specific engine branches. Legal actions and spectator explanations derive from the same committed definitions and facts." }

priority "tactics-and-strategy" { statement "Every system supports fast immediate decisions and slower meaningful commitments or consequences: production and specialization, trade offers and networks, research choices and programs, political responses and institutions, movement and campaigns. Calendar, strategic cadence and visual animation are separate concerns." }
